From d79870233ac0b90c63e172cfa196ac822e2107d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yumi-Lab <nicolas@yumi-lab.com>
Date: Mon, 20 Jul 2026 19:51:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/16] tcg/arm: inline jump-cache probe for guest indirect
 branches (user-mode)

At a DISAS_JUMP exit the cpu tuple (cs_base, flags, cflags) equals the
tuple the current TB was translated with, since any instruction that
changes the hflags ends its TB through another exit kind.  Emit an
inline probe of the per-cpu jump cache that validates the cached entry
entirely against translation-time constants: guest pc against cpu_pc,
and the cached TB's cs_base/flags/cflags against the current TB's own
tuple (the cflags compare also catches CF_INVALID).  On a hit, jump
straight to the cached TB's code; on any mismatch fall back to the
lookup helper, which stays the only writer of the cache.  BTYPE is the
one tuple bit that can change on the branch itself, so the probe is
only emitted when the translator knows BTYPE stays zero, and the
comparison constants have BTYPE cleared.

A first attempt that gated cache fills on a per-cpu canonical tuple and
flushed on change was abandoned after measurement: the guest alternates
tuples between code communities (815 k changes per boot), which turned
the flush policy into a cache-thrash storm.  This version keeps the
upstream fill semantics untouched.

Measured on the reference board: torn64 throughput +48 percent, guest
runtime boot 13 s to 10 s cold and 8 s to 6 s with the persistent
translation cache, batteries green.  The saved work per dispatch is the
helper call itself: state recompute, hash and compares, some 40 host
instructions replaced by about 20 inline ones on the hit path.
